{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Head-Worn Display.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Head-Worn Display.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"head worn display head worn display","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Head-Worn Display.md","fileName":"Head-Worn Display.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Head-Worn Display","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 13:11\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[XR Display Systems]] [[XR Environments]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["XR Display Systems","XR Environments","Visual Display","Virtual Reality","Augmented Reality","Field of Regard","Field of View","Latency","Optical See-Through Display","Video See-Through Display","Ocularity","3duserinterfaces2ande.pdf","augmentedreality_pearson.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Head-Worn Display.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Head-Worn Display.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"head worn display head worn display","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Head-Worn Display.md","fileName":"Head-Worn Display.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Head-Worn Display","heading":"Head-Worn Display","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A head-worn display is a [[Visual Display]] worn on the user's head so the imagery remains aligned with the eyes as the head moves. Opaque versions can immerse a user in [[Virtual Reality]], while see-through forms can overlay information for [[Augmented Reality]].\n\nHead tracking lets the system render from the user's changing viewpoint. Because the display turns with the head, it can provide a 360-degree [[Field of Regard]] even when its instantaneous [[Field of View]] is much smaller.\n\nDesign tradeoffs include resolution, weight, comfort, optics, depth-cue conflicts, and [[Latency]].\n\nFor AR, a head-worn display may use an [[Optical See-Through Display|optical see-through]] combiner or a [[Video See-Through Display|video see-through]] camera pipeline. Monocular, bi-ocular, and binocular arrangements provide different [[Ocularity|ocularity]], while eye-to-display calibration and focus determine how well imagery aligns with and can be comfortably viewed beside physical objects.","tags":["baby"],"links":["XR Display Systems","XR Environments","Visual Display","Virtual Reality","Augmented Reality","Field of Regard","Field of View","Latency","Optical See-Through Display","Video See-Through Display","Ocularity","3duserinterfaces2ande.pdf","augmentedreality_pearson.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Head-Worn Display.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Head-Worn Display.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"head worn display head worn display","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Head-Worn Display.md","fileName":"Head-Worn Display.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Head-Worn Display","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[3duserinterfaces2ande.pdf]]\n[[augmentedreality_pearson.pdf]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["XR Display Systems","XR Environments","Visual Display","Virtual Reality","Augmented Reality","Field of Regard","Field of View","Latency","Optical See-Through Display","Video See-Through Display","Ocularity","3duserinterfaces2ande.pdf","augmentedreality_pearson.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""}]}